 # Neural Radiance Fields
-
 Also called: Explicit Radiance Field Representations
 
 ## Overview
-Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) represent a 3D scene as a continuous volumetric function that maps 3D spatial coordinates and 2D viewing directions to emitted RGB color and volume density [S1][S5][S10, unofficial]. It solves the problem of novel view synthesis by replacing explicit geometric primitives (meshes or point clouds) with an implicit neural representation encoded in network weights, enabling photorealistic rendering of complex scenes with view-dependent effects like specularities and transparency [S1][S2, unofficial][S14, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from its parent method [Radiance-field optimization](44-radiance-field-optimization.md) by introducing specific architectural choices (such as asymmetric MLPs or hierarchical sampling) and optimization strategies to make the implicit representation tractable for high-fidelity reconstruction.
